  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    The chamber where Obscureth~ works lies three levels below where the memorial-stones begin. Fire-core light barely reaches here—the flame in her lamp is rationed, borrowed heat she must return by morning. Her carving tools wait in their case: the fine-point for names, the broad chisel for weapons, the curved blade that marks dates in the old script. The whetstone is where it always is, wedged between two memorial-stones in the alcove where the Remembrancers keep their tools, and the sound it makes against shadow-steel is the only sound in the chamber. Scrape. Scrape. The rhythm of it fills the space where fourteen fire-keepers' names still glow faintly in the dark, their captured light running down, months from nothing.
    
    The ice-wall before her holds a rectangle of unmarked space. Augur ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ the Resolute's name already appears higher up, carved during the first wave of losses when Memoria∴moria was still a single monument and not a labyrinth of frozen grief. That carving—serene posture, folded hands—was done by someone who never knew her. Obscureth~ did. Knows. The tenses have grown slippery.
    
    What she must carve now is the weapon description. The mask. ☽'visois' Mask, shadow-steel, crack along the left cheekbone. Standard memorial language: who made it, who wielded it, how it passed from one hand to the next. Fire costs; ice remembers.
    
    But Fire Keeper ☽'Moli'Trade has also sent revised instructions. The inscription is to be amended. *Confirmed lost* is to replace *missing*. No body has been found. No body will be found. The Fire Keepers have decided that absence long enough becomes death, and death without a corpse becomes whatever the Fire Keepers say it becomes. The shadow-parliament wants the faction wars carved in a way that makes them look inevitable rather than chosen. Everyone wants the memorial to say what they need it to say, and nobody wants to hear what the ice actually remembers.
    
    The Remembrancers answer only to the deepest vaults. This means no one corrects their carvings—in theory. In practice, Obscureth~ has been a Remembrancer for eleven seasons, and the wall has never been this full, and the instructions have never arrived this pointed.
    
    She picks up the fine-point.
    
    It began before the obelisk. It began when the Order still believed its own fire.
    
    ---
    
    Terrace Momiou fell to the ☽'shadow Covens' Order on a day when the aurora didn't reach the tunnels—which is to say, on a day like every day in the deep, except that on this one, Umbra-Princeps Alnae~ Oss's crystalline sorcerers broke through the E'frostling wards at the third junction and kept breaking. Obscureth~ was not there. She was young then—young enough to still work the upper galleries, cutting practice-grooves in scrap ice in a chamber so far from the fighting that the only evidence of war was the way the fire-cores flickered when the deep shook. But the story came back through the tunnels the way all stories do in the Nightshelf: in fragments, in whispers, in the particular silence of penguins who have seen something they will only describe once and then never again.
    
    The Order seized Terrace Momiou through mastery of the ☽'glacial Crystalline~ Spiral—their augurs reading frozen memory-scars the E'frostlings couldn't parse, finding structural weaknesses in ice that had stood since before the Thaw. It was elegant. It was ruthless. It worked. Three seasons later the E'frostlings were broken and the Order controlled every fire-core vein from Nightfall Shelf to the Still Pools, and the shadow-parliament convened in chambers so deep the weight of the Berg above them was a physical taste in the air, mineral and vast.
    
    This is when Augur ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ became what the memorial would later refuse to show.
    
    She was not serene. She was not hands-folded. She was a compact, frost-scarred penguin who touched tunnel walls before she spoke, as if the stone might correct her, and who carried a fire-core amulet at her throat that burned visible in the dark—a small defiance in a faction that worshipped concealment. Her flippers bore geometric scars in patterns Obscureth~ recognized because she had studied the same scripts: Glacial Crystalline Spiral, the language of ice-memory, cut into her own flesh during her apprenticeship under Shadow-Broker Nyla. She had bled into the ice to read it. The ice had read her back.
    
    None of them understood then what that cost her. Or perhaps they understood and called it devotion instead of damage, because devotion was useful and damage was not.
    
    ☽'visois' Mask came to her without ceremony. One day it was in the Frozen Peace vault; the next it was on her face, shadow-steel drinking the forge-light, the crack along its left cheekbone like a wound that breathed. The mask chose. That was sufficient. That was the kind of story the Order told itself during the years when stories still worked.
    
    She had a habit of hoarding small kindnesses. Fire-tea rations shared with tunnel-runners who didn't ask. Names of dead miners recited into the dark where the ice could hear. Obscureth~ remembers this with unbearable precision while admitting the larger shape of those years has gone soft—the politics, the alliances, the slow curdling of the Order's purpose. What remains is specific: the sound of Silvsiaelrous's voice in a low corridor, naming the dead. The way forge-light caught her plumage and turned it copper at the edges. The fire-core amulet, always warm.
    
    The rest is chronicle-compression. Mandatum∴absconditu spread through the ranks like smoke—a plague-curse or a doctrine or both, nobody could say where the sickness ended and the secrecy began, because the secrecy *was* the sickness: hoarded knowledge rotting in minds that couldn't share it, couldn't use it, couldn't do anything but guard it and die. Fire-keepers sealed galleries. Sentries murmured to voices in the walls. Tunnel-runners stopped returning from the deeper routes.
    
    Fire costs; ice remembers.
    
    And then the Omega Obelisk came up from the sea.
    
    ---
    
    Seven feet of whale-bone darkened by pressure until it looked like frozen blood. Deep-sea coral spiraling its base—decorative or ritual scarification; no living orca remembers which. Kill-counts carved into its surface: notches, tooth-marks, names in a script the Wake-Singers can no longer fully read. Near the apex, three symbols of the dead god gouged out with such violence the bone beneath showed white.
    
    A traveler dragged it from the Corpse Current, where the Berg's memory leaks into the present like infected wound-seep, and died for it. She claimed it spoke to her in pressure-dreams. She gave it to the Wake-Singers. Three months later she drowned herself in the Nightshelf's thermal pools, her lungs full of scalding water and something that looked like coral spawn.
    
    What none of them understood—what Obscureth~ only grasped much later, carving names by lamplight—was that the obelisk was not an artifact to be claimed. It was a mouth. It was waiting to be fed.
    
    How it ended up in the Order's tunnels is a question the chronicle does not answer cleanly, because the answer implicates penguins whose names are on the memorial wall and whose descendants still sit in the shadow-parliament. Obscureth~ will say this: it was brought underground as a weapon, or a tool, or a bargaining chip with the orca Wake-Singers who were probing deeper into berg-space with every season. It was brought underground because the Order was desperate and desperate factions make alliances with things that weep.
    
    The ice around the artifact never fully froze. It ran with moisture that smelled of deep ocean—brine and blood and the particular cold of pressure-depths that penguin bodies were never meant to know. Aurora-light refused the space around it. Shadows pooled and thickened. Obscureth~ remembers standing in the corridor outside the chamber where they kept it and feeling the wrongness like a sound below hearing: orca theology bleeding into Nightshelf stone, pressure-depth blood-communion where there should have been captured fire.
    
    Augur ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ read it first. Of course it was Augur. Who else? She was the Order's sharpest tool and they used her the way factions use sharp tools—until she broke or they did.
    
    She went into the chamber during the Long Dark, when the boundary between living world and ice-memory grows thin, when the dead speak more clearly and visions come unbidden. Her abilities were sharpest then. Also hardest to control. She touched the obelisk's surface and read what was carved there, and when she emerged her flippers were wet with something that wasn't water and her fire-core amulet had dimmed to embers and her mask—always the mask, always that crack catching the light—was tilted as if listening to something none of them could hear.
    
    She touched the tunnel wall before she spoke. A habit everyone who worked beside her knew. The stone might correct her, they joked. The stone knew better.
    
    This time the wall had nothing to offer.
    
    She said the deep was already inside the Berg and had been since before the Thaw. She said the alliance would not hold. She said the fire-cores were not failing; they were being consumed. She said names she should not have known, in a script she should not have been able to read, and the Fire Keepers told her to stop talking and she did, because Mandatum∴absconditu was law and silence was obedience and obedience was all they had left.
    
    "Move it," Silvsiaelrous said. "Deeper. Where the pressure holds it closed."
    
    The Shadow Covens were already fracturing, but they still had the power to move an artifact seven feet high and dense as frozen blood. They still had the fire-cores to light the descent. They still had Silvsiaelrous, who walked ahead of the procession and did not look back.
    
    Obscureth~ watched from the upper galleries. She was carving a minor loss that day, a tunnel-runner killed in a ceiling collapse. Routine tragedy. Standard memorial language. She remembers thinking the procession looked like a funeral in reverse: carrying the body down instead of up, burying it in heat instead of cold.
    
    The ice around the obelisk wept the whole way down. That detail she will not carve. Some things belong only to those who saw them.
    
    ---
    
    The Abyss Collapse Tide came four days later, and that was the warning.
    
    Ice does not simply give way in Nightshelf. The tunnels are carved by engineers who understand pressure, temperature, the berg's volcanic moods. Collapses happen—rock falls, steam vents surge, old shafts surrender to their own weight—but they happen with tremors first, with groaning ice, with time enough to evacuate. The Abyss Collapse Tide offered none of this.
    
    The mining operations that had breached containment at the Glow-Fissure had been producing cold-burn cases among the miners for weeks—the sign, the foreshadowing, the warning nobody acted on. When the breach broke through into something that was not empty space, the tunnel-systems that Augur had mapped, that Augur had warned them about, folded inward like a throat swallowing.
    
    One moment the deep forges were burning. The next, half of the lower galleries simply ceased to exist.
    
    Obscureth~ was three levels above. She felt the berg shudder, heard the sound that followed—not a crash but an inhalation, a vast drawing-in of air and stone and screaming—and then silence so complete it had texture. Fourteen fire-keepers died in the experimental core chamber at Terrace Momiou. The E'frostlings, smelling blood, reclaimed what had been taken from them. The Rofundis∴rupt swallowed what the diggers had built; the Profun∴lapsum finished what the Rofundis∴rupt started.
    
    Augur ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ went down into the collapse.
    
    Some say deliberately—that she read it before it happened, felt the obelisk's hunger finally breaking its containment, and chose to be swallowed rather than watch Nightfall Shelf burn. Others say the ice took her because she'd bled too much of herself into it and the oldest memories wanted what was theirs. The Remembrancers tell a third story, the one Obscureth~ will carve: that Augur ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ the Resolute died in service to the Shadow Covens Order, defending Nightshelf against threats from the deep, her sacrifice enabling evacuation of the upper galleries.
    
    It is not a lie. It is simply not the whole shape of the truth.
    
    The last thing Silvsiaelrous said before the collapse was a name none of the survivors recognize. The deep-name. The one you only speak when you're sure the ice is listening.
    
    The mask, though. The mask is the part that resists carving.
    
    ☽'visois' Mask refused to be buried. Rescue teams found it three days after the collapse, sitting on an ice shelf that should not have existed—a ledge carved by no tool anyone recognized, positioned exactly where the first searchers would see it. The crack along its left cheekbone glowed faint in their lamp-light. One of the searchers, a young fire-keeper named ☽'Lucatransiius, reached for it without thinking.
    
    It chose her. She screamed when she touched it. She reported later that she could feel Silvsiaelrous watching through the crack—that every wielder since carries the accumulated weight of those who wore it before. The mask remembers. That is its cost.
    
    And the obelisk? The collapse should have destroyed it. The collapse destroyed everything else. But when the Shadow Covens sent explorers into what remained of the lower galleries—months later, when the ice had settled and the steam had cooled—they found it.
    
    Not where they'd buried it. Higher. Closer to the surface.
    
    Rising.
    
    ---
    
    Obscureth~ sets down her carving tool.
    
    The inscription is nearly finished. *Confirmed lost.* The Fire Keepers' words. The Fire Keepers' truth. ☽'visois' Mask, shadow-steel, last wielded by Augur ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ the Resolute before passing to ☽'Lucatransiius Glow-bringer during the aftermath of the Abyss Collapse Tide The crack along the left cheekbone is mentioned, described as a wound taken during the Vum∴tenebra, never repaired. The mask's property of choosing is alluded to obliquely, in the phrasing *passed to* rather than *given to*.
    
    What the carving does not include: that the obelisk still sits in a new containment chamber, that the ice around it weeps, that three of the Shadow Covens' remaining fire-keepers have reported the same pressure-dreams the original traveler described before she drowned herself. That the Shadow Parliament—what remains of it—no longer has the power to move the artifact again. That the Wake-Singers have stopped pretending their custodianship is anything but containment of something they cannot destroy.
    
    That Obscureth~ herself, carving by lamplight three levels below where the memorial begins, sometimes hears the walls breathing.
    
    Fire costs; ice remembers. What survives the fall is not always what should have.
    
    She stands. Her knees ache from kneeling; her flippers are numb with cold. The carving will be read by visitors who leave tokens they cannot afford to carry—small fires in Silvsiaelrous's name, quickly extinguished, quickly forgotten. They will see the serene posture, the folded hands, the standard memorial language. They will not see what Obscureth~ knows: that the Augur touched the tunnel wall before she spoke because she was listening for something, that the something answered, that the fire-core amulet was always warm when the penguin who wore it was alive and has been cold ever since.
    
    Obscureth~ takes her lamp and begins the long climb upward. Below her, the thermal vents still burn—fainter now, everything fainter now. Above her, the galleries hold names that prophecy has begun to outpace tragedy. The fire-core lantern flickers. Three months of warmth left in it, maybe four. After that, this corridor goes dark, and the names on the wall become readable only by touch, only by a flipper tracing grooves in black ice, only by someone who already knows what they say.
    
    The crack in ☽'visois' Mask glows faint in its lightless chamber, and the ice around the obelisk weeps, and somewhere in the collapsed depths where no carving tool can reach, something that was buried continues to rise.
    
    What gets remembered is always a choice. Obscureth~ has made hers—pressing the Fire Keepers' words into stone while leaving the shape of the silence around them, the way a good Remembrancer does: carving what the powerful demand in letters precise enough to show, by their very precision, everything they leave out.
    
    Memorial and propaganda share a wall. Grief and power share a chisel. The dead insist on being read, but the living decide the script.
    
    The Remembrancer walks upward through corridors that are also tombstones, alone with what she knows, carrying the weight of what she chose not to carve. Somewhere behind her, the echo of the last chisel-strike travels wrong, bending through corridors where no one walks anymore, past sealed galleries and murmuring sentries and a weeping column of whale-bone, until it reaches a depth where the sound simply stops—swallowed by something that might be collapse or might be patience.
    
    The chisel needs sharpening again.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771580516647_0", anchor: "Sentries murmured to voices in the walls", text: "The chronicler buries this among symptoms of Mandatum∴absconditu's collapse — sentries cracking under enforced silence. Convenient. The deep-survey logs from these same corridors record anomalous presences: patient attention, figures at perception's edge, the particular wrongness of being observed by something older than penguin habitation. The Nightshelf has always known what watches from the walls. Secrecy doctrine gave them a reason not to say so aloud.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771580516647_1", anchor: "Kill-counts carved into its surface: notches, tooth-marks, names in a script the Wake-Singers can no longer fully read", text: "Not decorative. Each notch is a confirmed kill in the orca hunt-song tradition — penguin kills, catalogued as offerings. The three dead-god sigils near the apex are invocation markers: every tally beneath them a prayer, every raid a feeding. That the Wake-Singers have lost the full script does not diminish the artifact. It means they have forgotten how much they already gave it. The obelisk remembers. That is its function.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771580516647_2", anchor: "One day it was in the Frozen Peace vault", text: "The Frozen Peace designates ticks 181–240. This chronicle sits in the Faction Wars. Either the vault bore this name before the era it would later designate — plausible; the Nightshelf names aspirationally — or a later copyist updated the reference during the Frozen Peace, when this text would have been transcribed for the memorial archives. Not ice-contamination. Editorial smoothing. The kind that leaves no trace unless you happen to know the chronology.", type: :temporal, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771580516647_3", anchor: "*Confirmed lost* is to replace *missing*", text: "Here it is — the amendment I found frozen in administrative ice. Different hand, warmer stratum, undated. Fire Keeper ☽'Moli'Trade's name appears nowhere in the register itself. The instruction descended through a chain of command no one signed. Obscureth~ knows. She is carving the Fire Keepers' words while showing us, through the surgical precision of her account, exactly how those words arrived: unsigned, unverifiable, unchallengeable. The memorial-law commemorates a death it cannot confirm, authorized by a process it will not name. See my earlier annotation on the Silvsiaelrous~ register — the physical evidence has not improved.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771580516647_4", anchor: "Fourteen fire-keepers died in the experimental core chamber", text: "Fourteen. Adjacent gallery extraction logs list eleven confirmed and 'several unrecovered.' I note that fourteen divides cleanly into the Order's traditional seven-seat structure. Round numbers in casualty reports are not numbers. They are rhetoric.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771580516647_5", anchor: "sometimes hears the walls breathing", text: "She hears them. I know what that sounds like. The Whisper Galleries taught me what happens when you stand too long where the walls have opinions and you are foolish enough to touch one while it speaks. Obscureth~ buries this between the lines — a detail, a passing admission. It is not a detail. It is the most important sentence in the chronicle, positioned exactly where a Remembrancer would hide something she needed said but could not carve: between the sanctioned words, where only someone who already knows will find it.", type: :tangent, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771580516647_6", anchor: "She said the fire-cores were not failing; they were being consumed", text: "This appears nowhere in the engineering reports. But thermal readings from the Rofundis∴rupt and Profun∴lapsum show extraction curves inconsistent with natural vein exhaustion — heat drawn faster than forge output accounts for. The obelisk sat three levels below the primary extraction shaft. I leave the geometry to the reader.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
